                                        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-neutral-900 mb-6">Why Disconnected Tools Stop Working <span style="background:linear-gradient(208deg, #09e179, #00d46e, #00c064    );-webkit-background-clip: text;-webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;">at Scale</span></h2>
                                        <p class="text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed mb-10">Best-of-breed tooling worked when Amazon was the only marketplace that mattered. Today, sellers run inventory across Amazon, Walmart, and increasingly Mirakl. When your scanner, repricer, inventory, and orders live in separate systems, three predictable failures show up.</p>
                                        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
                                            <div class="rounded-3xl border border-neutral-200 bg-white p-8 shadow-sm">
                                                <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 mb-3">1. Data Silos</h3>
                                                <p class="text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">Your scanner finds a product on a wholesale list. You manually enter it into your repricer. You upload it to inventory management. You check Walmart restrictions in a separate tool. Then you realize your pricing data hasn&#x27;t synced back to the scanner. Now you&#x27;re back in the repricer making manual edits. This workflow happens dozens of times per day, and every handoff is a chance to introduce a typo or skip a step.</p>
                                            </div>
                                            <div class="rounded-3xl border border-neutral-200 bg-white p-8 shadow-sm">
                                                <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 mb-3">2. Repricing Lag</h3>
                                                <p class="text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">Your sourcing tells you a product is available at one cost. Your repricer thinks it should sell at another. But your repricer doesn&#x27;t know what just arrived in inventory, and it doesn&#x27;t know what the current Buy Box looks like on the other channel you sell on. Hours later you realize you&#x27;ve been pricing without the latest competitive landscape. You adjust manually. The cycle repeats every time inventory or competition shifts.</p>
                                            </div>
                                            <div class="rounded-3xl border border-neutral-200 bg-white p-8 shadow-sm">
                                                <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 mb-3">3. Marketplace Alignment Failures</h3>
                                                <p class="text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">You set repricing rules on Amazon to target the Buy Box. You set different rules on Walmart because the fee structure and competitive dynamics are different. Your inventory tool doesn&#x27;t know which rules apply where. When stock levels shift, repricing on one channel can outrun availability on the other. You wake up to suppressed listings because the system oversold against a phantom number.</p>
                                            </div>
                                        </div>
                                        <div class="mt-10 rounded-3xl bg-neutral-100 border border-neutral-200 p-10">
                                            <p class="text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed text-center">Ecom Circles fixes the root cause: sourcing, repricer, inventory, and orders share the same data, so changes in one place show up everywhere they need to.</p>
                                        </div>
                                    </div>
                                </div>
