                            <section class="py-20 md:py-28 px-6 bg-neutral-50 bg-shades">
                                <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
                                    <div style="opacity:1;transform:translateY(24px)">
                                        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-neutral-900 mb-6">The All-in-One Argument </h2>
                                        <p class="text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">Five years ago, the best Amazon sellers used best-of-breed. Twelve tools, each great at one thing. Manual handoffs were tolerable because Amazon was the only marketplace that mattered, and the workflows were predictable.</p>
                                        <p
                                        class="mt-4 text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">That broke when Walmart scaled. Now sourcing has to consider both marketplaces; repricing has to handle different fee structures and competitive dynamics; inventory has to reflect what&#x27;s actually available
                                            where. Twelve tools become twenty-four data points to reconcile, and the time you spend reconciling is time you&#x27;re not sourcing or shipping.</p>
                                            <p class="mt-4 text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">All-in-one isn&#x27;t the trendy option. It&#x27;s the option that lets you scale across channels without burning hours every week on data entry and tool-switching. Ecom Circles was built by sellers who hit
                                                that wall and decided the right answer was to merge the toolset, not just add another integration.</p>
                                            <p class="mt-4 text-neutral-600 leading-relaxed">You can keep buying separate subscriptions and managing the chaos. Or you can pick one platform built for the way you actually sell across Amazon, Walmart, and beyond.</p>
                                    </div>
